# Copy to .env before running the app:  cp .env.example .env
#
# Every variable here is validated by src/lib/env.ts at boot. A missing or
# malformed value stops the server starting — it does not surface later as a
# runtime error on someone's contribution.
#
# In development the values below are also the built-in defaults, so a clean
# clone runs without this file. In production there are no defaults and every
# variable is required.

# Public origin of the app. Used for share links and OG image URLs, so it must
# be the externally reachable origin, not a container-internal one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Database -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two roles, and the difference matters. isipheko_app is what the application
# connects as; it holds INSERT and SELECT on ledger_entries and deliberately not
# UPDATE or DELETE. isipheko_owner owns the schema and runs migrations.
#
# Pointing DATABASE_URL at the owner role would leave the app able to rewrite
# the ledger, silently, with every test still passing. Do not do it to make
# something work locally.
#
# Port 5433, not 5432 — see compose.yaml.

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://isipheko_app:isipheko_local_dev@localhost:5433/isipheko
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://isipheko_owner:isipheko_local_dev@localhost:5433/isipheko

# --- Key material -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both are 32 bytes, base64. Generate with:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# The values below are development-only and are worthless by design — they
# protect a throwaway local database and are published in this file. Production
# refuses to start if it finds either of them, so they cannot reach a real
# deployment by being forgotten.
#
# STOPGAP: architecture §10 and §7.3 put both of these in a KMS, held separately
# from the database credential. They are environment variables only until that
# decision is taken. See docs/decisions.md M1-02.

# AES-256-GCM key for the bank account number column.
BANK_ACCOUNT_ENCRYPTION_KEY=ZGV2ZWxvcG1lbnQtb25seS1rZXktZG8tbm90LXVzZSE=

# Pepper for the ID number hash. Rotating this invalidates every stored hash,
# so it is generated once and kept.
ID_NUMBER_PEPPER=ZGV2ZWxvcG1lbnQtb25seS1wZXBwZXItZG8tbm90ISE=

# Peppers the one-time codes, and the phone/IP/user-agent hashes in the audit
# log. A six-digit code is a 10^6 space — an unpeppered hash of one is a lookup
# table, so this is what makes a stolen otp_challenges row useless.
#
# Separate from ID_NUMBER_PEPPER deliberately: rotating this costs the codes in
# flight, rotating that invalidates every stored identity hash.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
OTP_PEPPER=ZGV2ZWxvcG1lbnQtb25seS1vdHAtcGVwcGVyLWRvISE=

# Who may open the report review queue at /review (M3-07). Comma-separated
# phone numbers, in any form the app accepts — +27821234567, 0821234567.
#
# In the environment rather than in a database column deliberately: the
# application role holds UPDATE on `organisers`, so an is_admin column would be
# a privilege the application could grant itself. This one it can only read.
#
# A stopgap shape, not a design. See docs/decisions.md M3-07 §2.
# Empty means nobody, which is valid — the app starts and the queue goes unread.
ADMIN_PHONE_NUMBERS=

# --- PayFast (checkout only) --------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional. Unset means PayFast is not wired, and the app starts fine without
# it — payFastProvider() throws at the point of use and names what is missing.
# Making these required in production would refuse to start a deployment that
# is not using PayFast at all.
#
# Merchant ID and key are on the PayFast merchant dashboard. The passphrase is
# under Settings, "Salt Passphrase", and is not optional: without one the
# signature is a checksum over data the sender chose rather than a shared
# secret.
#
# The values below are PayFast's own published sandbox credentials.
# PAYFAST_MODE is anything-but-live = sandbox, deliberately — defaulting the
# other way is how a test transaction reaches a real card.
#
# **PayFast is checkout only.** Their General Terms 5.17(v) and (vi) forbid
# aggregating for multiple suppliers and submitting a transaction on behalf of a
# third party, so this settles to one merchant account — ours — and holds no
# balance for an organiser. See src/adapters/payments/payfast-provider.ts.

PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID=10000100
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_KEY=46f0cd694581a
PAYFAST_PASSPHRASE=jt7NOE43FZPn
PAYFAST_MODE=sandbox

# --- Object storage -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Optional, and development or test only. Where the local object store writes;
# unset means `.cache/objects` under the working directory.
#
# There is no production object store, and objectStore() refuses to construct
# one rather than falling back to local disk (OPS-09). That refusal is not
# fussiness: M4-01 keeps no original of a contributor's photograph, only the
# four re-encodes, so one written to a container's disk is lost on the next
# deploy and unreadable from every other instance before that.

# OBJECT_STORE_DIR=
